    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d440">

                <pb facs="0516" n="412" xml:id="pg412"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">832.00 Revolutions/319: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in Brazil</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Thurston</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1932-08-12T17:00:00-04:00">August 12, 1932—5
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>62. Your 84, August 12, noon. The Department sees no objection to your giving
                    such informal assistance as you may judge advisable in connection with the
                    efforts of American citizens who desire to leave Santos by steamer.</p>
                <p>It is obvious that if arrangements are made, with the approval of the Brazilian
                    Government, for an American vessel to enter the harbor at Santos, satisfactory
                    assurances will have to be obtained from the insurgents that they will permit
                    the vessel to leave.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
